# /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh writes the VM dirty-page ratios.
#
# Lenovo ships the script and its own init stanza:
#
#     /vendor/etc/init/tn_vendor_performance.rc:5
#         service tn_performance /vendor/bin/sh /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh
#
# and file_contexts gives /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh no exec label of
# its own, so it lands in the catch-all vendor_qti_init_shell domain. Measured on
# flash 2, permissive:
#
#     avc: denied { write } for comm="sh" name="dirty_background_ratio"
#          path="/proc/sys/vm/dirty_background_ratio"
#          scontext=u:r:vendor_qti_init_shell:s0
#          tcontext=u:object_r:proc_dirty:s0 tclass=file permissive=1
#     avc: denied { open }  for the same path
#
# ★ STOCK GRANTS THIS AND THIS BUILD DOES NOT — the gap is ours, not Qualcomm's.
# From the factory image's own policy
# (work/unpacked/parts/vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_sepolicy.cil):
#
#     (allow vendor_qti_init_shell proc_dirty_202404 (file (write)))
#     (allow tn_vendor_performance_shell proc_dirty_202404 (file (write)))
#
# Two things follow. First, the rule below is parity with the shipping device
# rather than an invention. Second, stock also carries a DEDICATED
# tn_vendor_performance_shell domain for this script, which is the better model —
# a Lenovo tuning script should not need the catch-all init-shell domain — and it
# is not reproduced here because it needs an exec label, a domain and a
# file_contexts entry for a script whose whole job is four sysctl writes. If this
# device tree ever grows a proper domain for the TN scripts, this rule comes out
# with them.
#
# `open` is granted alongside `write` because the denial pair shows both, and a
# write with no open is not a thing a shell can do. Stock lists only write; its
# policy version (proc_dirty_202404) predates the open-permission split that this
# build's platform policy applies.
allow vendor_qti_init_shell proc_dirty:file { open write };

# ⚠️ `allow vendor_qti_init_shell sysfs:file rw_file_perms;` WAS HERE AND IS
# GONE, session 34. It was the single broadest grant in this port's policy and it
# reached nothing.
#
# ★ It was the ONLY rule in the whole 30,634-line built vendor policy granting
# write to plain `sysfs:file`. Measured:
#
#     grep -cE '\(allow [a-z_0-9]+ sysfs_202504 \(file \(.*write' \
#         out/…/vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_sepolicy.cil     ->  1
#
# It was written for a denial attributed to `tn_vendor_performance.sh`, and four
# independent measurements say that attribution was wrong and the grant was
# unnecessary:
#
#  1. ★ tn_vendor_performance.sh IS NOT IN THIS DOMAIN. On the device it is
#     `u:object_r:vendor_file:s0`, not `vendor_qti_init_shell_exec`. It never ran
#     here. The eight scripts that ARE labelled for this domain are the five
#     `init.qcom.cabl.*`, the two `init.qcom.svi*` and `init.class_main.sh`, and
#     every one of them is a one-liner: seven call `ppd "cabl:…"` / `ppd "svi:…"`
#     (a separate binary in its own domain) and the last only reads and sets
#     properties. Not one writes to sysfs at all.
#
#  2. Every node the rule was justified by carries a SPECIFIC label, so plain
#     `sysfs` was never the type in play — `/sys/block/sda/queue/read_ahead_kb`
#     is `vendor_sysfs_sd`, `/sys/class/kgsl/kgsl/*` is `vendor_sysfs_kgsl`,
#     `/sys/block/dm-*/queue/*` is `sysfs_dm`, and each already had a narrower
#     QTI rule behind it.
#
#  3. The writes land. Live values are read_ahead_kb 512, page_reclaim_per_call
#     38400, swappiness 100 — none of them kernel defaults — and `init.qcom.rc`
#     plus `init.qti.kernel.rc` are what set read_ahead, i.e. init, not a shell.
#
#  4. `/sys/block/mmcblk0` does not exist on this tablet (UFS only, no microSD),
#     so two of the six paths the rule was costed against are for hardware that
#     is not here.
#
# ★★ And the argument the comment made for keeping it is the one this repo's own
# lesson 4 exists to refuse: "Parity with the shipping device, not an invention …
# so the gap is ours." Stock parity is the default, not the goal, and three of
# session 25's fixes were things stock gets wrong too. The very same comment then
# refused 38 other stock rules on the grounds that "granting access to things
# that are not there is how a policy stops meaning anything" — which is exactly
# what this rule was. The principle was right and was not applied to the line it
# was written above.
#
# If a future Lenovo tuning script does need sysfs, give it its own domain and
# its own narrow types, which is what the paragraph above this one already says.
